1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:10,400
Section 4 You will hear part of a lecture for business students about business values.

2
00:00:10,920 --> 00:00:40,021
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

3
00:00:40,541 --> 00:01:14,320
[BLANK_AUDIO] Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

4
00:01:14,394 --> 00:01:19,520
In public discussion of business, we take certain values for granted.

5
00:01:19,593 --> 00:01:22,720
Today I'm going to talk about four of them.

6
00:01:22,838 --> 00:01:29,000
Collaboration, hard work, creativity and excellence.

7
00:01:29,063 --> 00:01:31,945
Most people would say they're all good things.

8
00:01:32,008 --> 00:01:36,440
I'm going to suggest that's an over-simple view.

9
00:01:36,503 --> 00:01:44,028
The trouble with these values is that their theoretical concepts removed from the reality of day-to-day business.

10
00:01:44,109 --> 00:01:50,360
Pursue values, by all means, but be prepared for what may happen as a result.

11
00:01:50,446 --> 00:01:56,120
They can actually cause damage, which is not at all the intention.

12
00:01:56,176 --> 00:02:01,822
Business leaders generally try to do the right thing, but all too often the right thing backfires,

13
00:02:01,822 --> 00:02:09,100
if those leaders adopt values without understanding and managing the side effects that arise.

14
00:02:09,164 --> 00:02:13,340
The values can easily get in the way of what's actually intended.

15
00:02:13,449 --> 00:02:20,300
Okay, so the first value I'm going to discuss is collaboration.

16
00:02:20,389 --> 00:02:22,780
Let me give you an example.

17
00:02:22,843 --> 00:02:25,890
On a management training course I once attended,

18
00:02:25,890 --> 00:02:33,000
we were put into groups and had to construct a bridge across a stream using building blocks that we were given.

19
00:02:33,072 --> 00:02:40,467
The rule was that everyone in the team had to move at least one building block during the construction.

20
00:02:40,538 --> 00:02:47,000
This was intended to encourage team work, but it was really a job best done by one person.

21
00:02:47,065 --> 00:02:53,000
The other teams tried to collaborate on building the structure and descended into confusion,

22
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
with everyone getting in each other's way.

23
00:02:56,080 --> 00:03:00,000
Our team leader solved the challenge brilliantly.

24
00:03:00,066 --> 00:03:12,200
She simply asked everyone in the team to move a piece of few centimetres to comply with the rule and then let the person in the team with an aptitude for puzzles like this build it alone.

25
00:03:12,291 --> 00:03:15,400
We finished before any other team.

26
00:03:15,481 --> 00:03:20,560
My point is that the task wasn't really suited to team working.

27
00:03:20,702 --> 00:03:23,400
So why make it one?

28
00:03:23,491 --> 00:03:29,683
Teamwork can also lead to inconsistency, a common cause of poor sales.

29
00:03:29,761 --> 00:03:38,496
In the case of a smart phone that a certain company launched, one director wanted to target the business market,

30
00:03:38,496 --> 00:03:42,280
and another demanded it was aimed at consumers.

31
00:03:42,345 --> 00:03:51,581
The company wanted both directors to be involved, so gave the product a consumer-friendly name, but marketed it to companies.

32
00:03:51,663 --> 00:03:55,429
The result was that it met the needs of Niser Group.

33
00:03:55,492 --> 00:04:07,000
It would have been better to let one director or the other have his way, not both.

34
00:04:07,145 --> 00:04:12,360
Now, "industry-ness" or "hard work".

35
00:04:12,435 --> 00:04:16,040
It's easy to mock people who say they work hard.

36
00:04:16,116 --> 00:04:22,440
After all, a hamster running around in a wheel is working hard and getting nowhere.

37
00:04:22,508 --> 00:04:27,000
Of course, hard work is valuable, but only when properly targeted,

38
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:36,000
otherwise it wastes the resources that companies value most, time and energy, and that's bad for the organisation.

39
00:04:36,088 --> 00:04:46,000
There's a management model that groups people according to four criteria, clever, hardworking, stupid, and lazy.

40
00:04:46,082 --> 00:04:53,000
Here, lazy means having a rational determination not to carry out unnecessary tasks.

41
00:04:53,061 --> 00:04:56,000
It doesn't mean trying to avoid work altogether.

42
00:04:56,080 --> 00:05:05,200
Most people display two of these characteristics, and the most valuable people are those who are both clever and lazy.

43
00:05:05,278 --> 00:05:11,200
They possess intellectual clarity and they don't rush into making decisions.

44
00:05:11,270 --> 00:05:18,200
They come up with solutions to save the time and energy spent by the stupid and hard working group.

45
00:05:18,273 --> 00:05:26,400
Instead of throwing more man-hours at a problem, the clever and lazy group looks for a more effective solution.

46
00:05:26,494 --> 00:05:29,122
Next, we come to creativity.

47
00:05:29,216 --> 00:05:38,930
This often works well, creating an attention-grabbing TV commercial, for example, might lead to increased sales.

48
00:05:39,006 --> 00:05:41,504
But it isn't always a good thing.

49
00:05:41,579 --> 00:05:48,971
Some advertising campaigns are remembered for their creativity without having any effect on sales.

50
00:05:49,044 --> 00:05:53,796
This happened a few years ago with the launch of a chocolate bar.

51
00:05:53,869 --> 00:06:00,865
Subsequent research showed that plenty of consumers remembered the adverts, but had no idea what was being advertised.

52
00:06:00,930 --> 00:06:11,880
The trouble is that the creator derives pleasure from coming up with the idea and wrongly assumes the audience for the campaign will share that feeling.

53
00:06:11,957 --> 00:06:20,431
A company that brings out thousands of new products may seem more creative than a company that only has a few,

54
00:06:20,431 --> 00:06:24,597
but it may be too creative and make smaller profits.

55
00:06:24,676 --> 00:06:31,200
Creativity needs to be targeted to solve a problem that the company has identified.

56
00:06:31,267 --> 00:06:36,600
Just coming up with more and more novel products isn't necessarily a good thing.

57
00:06:36,776 --> 00:06:41,000
And finally, excellence.

58
00:06:41,082 --> 00:06:50,120
We all know companies that claim they strive for excellence, but it takes a long time to achieve excellence.

59
00:06:50,199 --> 00:06:57,120
In business, being first with a product is more profitable than having the best product.

60
00:06:57,191 --> 00:07:01,764
A major study of company performance compared pioneers, that is,

61
00:07:01,764 --> 00:07:07,547
companies bringing out the first version of a particular product, with followers,

62
00:07:07,547 --> 00:07:12,400
the companies that copied and improved on that product.

63
00:07:12,475 --> 00:07:18,880
The study found that the pioneers commanded an average market share of 29%.

64
00:07:18,954 --> 00:07:23,309
While the followers achieved less than half that, only 13%.

65
00:07:23,383 --> 00:07:27,000
Even though their product might have been better.

66
00:07:27,095 --> 00:07:38,000
Insisting on excellence in everything we do is time consuming, waste's energy and leads to losing out on opportunities.

67
00:07:38,079 --> 00:07:43,000
Sometimes second-rate work is more worthwhile than excellence.

68
00:07:43,082 --> 00:07:49,251
Make sure it's excellent, sounds like a good approach to business,

69
00:07:49,251 --> 00:07:57,080
but the just get started approach is likely to be more successful.

70
00:07:57,430 --> 00:08:07,588
That is the end of section 4.

71
00:08:07,938 --> 00:08:25,100
You now have half a minute to check your answers.

72
00:08:25,450 --> 00:08:38,760
That is the end of the listening test.